More generously than you, apparently. :) There is also a difference
between definitively preventable (that is: "if you had done X, you
would definitely have survived") and statistically preventable ("doing
X provides a high chance of survival"). For example, in the soldier
ant case you mentioned, leaving aside the do-you-go-to-the-mines-or-not
question, the chance of two Elbereths failing in a row[1] is .3^2 or 9%
(the chance of both succeeding is 49%, of one but not the other succeeding
42%); if you make that three Elbereths it's 2.7%, and so on. So even if
you don't allow yourself other survival mechanisms, Elbereth can solve
a large number of your problems a large portion of the time --- and a
larger portion of the time the more HP or AC you have to absorb failures.

Other solutions are similarly mostly-useful: Teleporting _might_ put you
somewhere worse, teleporting the monster might put it right next to you
again, &c. But we don't suggest that players not use those solutions.
We suggest that players use their tools before they're having a problem.
If you Really Want To Win Every Game, when you get dropped down a trap
door into a dangerous and unknown level, you write a bunch of Elbereths on
a square you can get back to before you start exploring. Is this boring?
Yes. Will it increase your ascension percentage? Quite likely.

Here's one problem I see with NetHack: Especially but not only because
of Elbereth, optimal early-game play is boring and involves pushing the
E button over and over again. This doesn't really depend on race, class,
alignment, gender, or what items you find early on.  

